A||||5||Bens88GT||Is there any way to raise the driver's seat 2"?||||5||
Z||000000||Bens88GT||02-11-2000||03:06 PM||||I just got my first Fiero, an 88GT, and I feel like an old granny trying to see over the dashboard. Right now I'm sitting on a cushion to raise me up, but I want to raise the seat itself. Any easy ways to do this?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ben||||reg||5
Z||000001||RZURCTN||02-11-2000||04:50 PM||||You could get a peice of threaded rod and extend your current studs.  Use washers or a 2" spacer to get your height.  You may also want to look into  spacing the rails from the seat, or find a different set of seats all together.  There are many ways, whatever you feel comfortable with.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-11-2000||05:17 PM||||Apparently Geo Storm seats will work.  Eddie said that they sit higher.  I guess you'd have to do some modifications such as welding some extra brackets to the seat rails and replacing the mounting bolts, but they look nice.  (See "Geo Storm seats are in!")||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-11-2000||05:23 PM||||For 2 inches you can get some 2" channel. Use steel channel not aluminum. Drill holes in the flanges, bolt the seat to the channel and then bolt the channel to the floor on the existing, unaltered studs. Plus with channel you could also move the seat forward more if you need it.<P>You can extend the studs and put spacers under the rails but it's harder to do that way. Also not very stable if not done carefully.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||02-11-2000||09:06 PM||||Cave dwellers must think alike. I used that steel channel method on a streetrod of mine and it worked great. The seats are very solid. Use hardened bolts (grade 8) so that they won't snap off in case of an accident.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bens88GT||02-12-2000||12:20 AM||||Hey guys, thanks for the ideas! The channel sounds good. Maybe I'll get to do that this weekend.<P>-Ben||||reg||7
